  "question": {
    "text": "A positive point charge is placed at <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></math> in front of an earthed metal sheet <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi></math>.&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Q</mi></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math> are two points between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi></math> as shown in the figure. If the electric field strength at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Q</mi></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math> are respectively <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mtext>E</mtext><mtext>Q</mtext></msub></math>, <math><msub><mrow><mtext>E</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></msub></math> and potential at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Q</mi></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math> are respectively <math><msub><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></msub></math>, <math><msub><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></msub></math>. Then<br />\r<br />\r<img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\qt0051_0d339d42cc49.jpg\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 109px;\" />"
  },
